  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of Today through Saturday...\n\nCold front moving through central zones at mid-day, will continue\nto quickly push through eastern MT and into the Dakotas by 6pm\nthis evening. Along the front showers and scattered thunderstorms\nare forecast, with a few stronger storms producing wind gusts\npossibly over 70 mph. Latest hi-res/CAM models continue to show\npockets of 70+mph winds (some significantly higher) over portions\nof SE MT (Carter/Fallon/Custer/Powder River counties) though each\nrun is placing these in different locations within these counties.\nSPC continues to advertise a Slight Risk for severe thunderstorms\nthis afternoon for this area, and would anticipate a Severe\nThunderstorm Watch should the models continue to show the strong\nwind signatures over the next few hours. \n\nAlready seeing clearing and the onset of more sustained windy\nconditions developing over the western foothills. Expect afternoon\nwesterly winds gusting 25-40 mph to become common this afternoon\ninto the early evening as subsidence behind the front drives down\nstronger winds aloft to the surface. \n\nClearing skies and decreasing winds expected tonight. Expect\ntemperatures below freezing over area mountains tonight, with\nwestern valleys dropping into the upper 30s to lower 40s. The rest\nof the area will see lows around 50 degrees tonight. \n\nMostly sunny, warmer, and dry conditions expected tomorrow. Winds\nwill be gusty out of the northwest over southeast MT early in the\nday, but wind speeds will decrease through the afternoon. \n\nTuesday through Thursday show considerable uncertainty. A strong\nPacific low will set up shop along the PacNW coast during this\nperiod. What happens during this period will depend greatly on \nenergy rotating around this system and how close it comes to out\narea. Currently a weak series of disturbances come northeast out \nof the Great Basin on Tuesday, providing more cloud cover and a \nlow to moderate chances for mainly afternoon/evening showers and \nthunderstorms focused on the western mountains and the MT/WY \nborder areas. A stronger negatively tilted trof is taking shape, \nin at least the GFS solutions, for Wednesday that if it becomes \nthe preferred solution could bring a much broader period of \nshowers and thunderstorms, including a chance for a few strong to \nsevere storms. Thursday sets up similar to Tuesday in the latest \nmodels. Overall will likely continue to see the models adjust to \nthe intricacies of the upper low position and strength, with \nvariability in the forecast as a result. For now the NBM forecast \nis for warming temperatures (80s-lower 90s) by Wednesday with at \nleast low chances for mainly afternoon and evening convection. But\nstay tuned as this may change going forward. \n\nFor Friday into the weekend, again this period will depend greatly\non how the evolution of the PacNW low works out. Low level south-\neast flow should allow for additional moisture to work into the\narea. Divergent south-southwest flow aloft should provide shear\nand dynamic lift at times. This pattern favors continued chances\nfor showers and thunderstorms, perhapse a few stronger storms,\nduring this period. Latest models start to track the upper low\ncloser to the area Saturday into Sunday, thus would anticipate\nincreasing precipitation chances with temperatures trending lower\nas the weekend progresses. Again, if you have outdoor plans this\nweek into next weekend, check back in on the forecast as there are\nlikely to be changes as the models work out the specific details\nwith this PacNW storm system. Chambers",
